# The full container run at 8a52ff0a: 34 gates, 2 red, and both reds were mine (2026-10-04)

`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --full` was run end to end against the committed tree at
`8a52ff0a`, the commit that added the `timeout children` gate and the test that installs a
`git` shim. It failed: `FAILED gates: cargo clippy cargo test`, exit 1. Both failures were
defects in the test shipped by that same commit, one of which could only be seen by this run.
This log records the verdict, the two failures as printed, what the green parts of the run
still establish, and why the CI run of the same push did not reach the second failure.

The tree here is not the fixed tree. The fixes are in the commit that follows and are recorded
in their own log; nothing below should be read as the state of `main`.

The full 3.0 MB / 35 837-line transcript is not committed. Every line quoted below is copied
out of it, and section 6 gives the commands to reproduce it and to re-measure the counts.

== 0. Why the run sits in its own clone

Same arrangement as `docs/verification/verify-in-docker-full-a04e91a7-2026-10-04.log` section
0, for the same reason: the script bind-mounts the repository root it was invoked from and
fingerprints the working tree before and after, so the run went to a self-contained clone
rather than to a checkout being edited underneath it.

    $ git clone <this repository> /var/tmp/verify-frozen-a04e91a7
    $ cd /var/tmp/verify-frozen-a04e91a7 && git checkout --detach 8a52ff0a
    $ IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --full \
          > /var/tmp/verify-full-8a52ff0a.log 2>&1; echo "DOCKER-FULL-EXIT=$?" \
          > /var/tmp/verify-full-8a52ff0a.exit

The clone's `git status --porcelain` printed nothing before the run and prints nothing after
it, which is what makes the checksum below a claim about `8a52ff0a`. The clone directory is
named after the commit it was cut for (`a04e91a7`) and was re-detached onto `8a52ff0a` for
this run; a clone carries its own object store, so the preflight accepts it.

== 1. The verdict, as printed

    == building chaos-verify:frozen from docker.io/library/debian:bookworm-slim
    == preparing cargo cache volumes
    == source tree: 4001 files, checksum 1801880867

    == preflight: container can read the repository
    preflight: OK

    == toolchain matches the pin
    rustc 1.94.0 (4a4ef493e 2026-03-02)
    cargo 1.94.0 (85eff7c80 2026-01-15)

    == pinned protoc launcher
    libprotoc 29.3

    ...

    GUI protocol types are up to date

    FAILED gates: cargo clippy cargo test

and the exit code, from the `echo` appended to the command rather than from the script:

    $ cat /var/tmp/verify-full-8a52ff0a.exit
    DOCKER-FULL-EXIT=1

The run did not print `UNATTRIBUTABLE`. The script recomputes the same checksum after the last
gate and prints that sentence only on a mismatch, so its absence is the statement that all 34
gates, red and green alike, belong to the tree whose checksum is 1801880867 and that no file
moved while they ran:

    $ grep -c UNATTRIBUTABLE /var/tmp/verify-full-8a52ff0a.log
    0

Elapsed time was 1 410 seconds (23 minutes 30 seconds), from the transcript's creation to its
last write, measured from the file's own timestamps rather than from the transcript:

    $ stat -c 'birth=%w mtime=%y' /var/tmp/verify-full-8a52ff0a.log

That is not a cold-cache figure and it is not a baseline. The cargo registry, the cargo git
checkouts and `target/` live in three named volumes that earlier runs had already filled, and
the image layers were reused. A run that stops early also costs less than one that runs every
leg to completion, so 23 minutes here is not comparable with 25 minutes for the green run.

== 2. What ran, and what the green part still establishes

`bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --list` in that clone prints 34 gate names, which is the
`gates=()` array of `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` at that commit. The transcript carries 41
`== ` lines: the 34 gates, 4 preamble lines printed before the array is entered (`building
chaos-verify:frozen`, `preparing cargo cache volumes`, `source tree:`, `preflight:`) and 3
sub-headings that two gates print for themselves. Two of the 34 went red; the other 32 passed,
and `--no-fail-fast` at the gate level is what let them all be measured in one pass instead of
the sweep stopping at the first red.

Three of the green gates print a count worth recording, because they are the ones that read the
whole tree:

    ignored-test baseline: 429 total, 0 approved bare, 0 new
    ignored-tests: all 429 ignored attributes carry a reason
    ignored-tests: baseline matches; 429 ignored attributes total
    metric labels hold: 83 registrations, 155 label-value call sites, 0 recorded

and the gate added by this very commit passed, including its own fixtures:

    == timeout children
    ...
    Ran 36 tests in 28.573s

    OK (skipped=2)
    timeout children hold: 12 timeout-abandoned output/status site(s), 12 kill their child on drop, 0 are std commands, 0 recorded

That line is the claim the gate exists to make: every `tokio::time::timeout(.., cmd.output())`
or `.status()` site in the tree marks its child `kill_on_drop(true)`. It passed here, and it
passes on the fixed tree, and it is independent of both red legs.

== 3. First red: `cargo clippy`, two lints in the test added by this commit

`== cargo clippy` runs `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings`, and
`-D warnings` turned two style lints into build errors. Both are in
`goal_classifier_tests.rs`, in the test this commit added:

    error: this `if` statement can be collapsed
        --> crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/session/goal_classifier_tests.rs:3680:9
    ...
         = note: `-D clippy::collapsible-if` implied by `-D warnings`

    error: this loop could be written as a `while let` loop
        --> crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/session/goal_classifier_tests.rs:3695:5
    ...
         = note: `-D clippy::while-let-loop` implied by `-D warnings`

    error: could not compile `xai-grok-shell` (lib test) due to 2 previous errors

`== cargo check`, the leg before it, had passed on the same source:

    == cargo check
    ...
        Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized] target(s) in 2m 06s

so this is not a build problem and not a type error; it is that the code compiles and does not
satisfy the lint set, which only the clippy leg applies. The host sweep does not run clippy
without `--with-build`, which is exactly the gap recorded in `docs/ci-test-debt.md`.

== 4. Second red: `cargo test`, one failure, and it was my own test poisoning the binary

The test leg is `cargo test --workspace --locked --no-fail-fast`, and across its 386
`test result:` blocks it came to one failure:

    $ grep -c '^test result:' /var/tmp/verify-full-8a52ff0a.log
    386
    $ grep -c '^test result: FAILED' /var/tmp/verify-full-8a52ff0a.log
    1
    $ grep -oE "^test result: (ok|FAILED)\. [0-9]+ passed; [0-9]+ failed; [0-9]+ ignored" \
          /var/tmp/verify-full-8a52ff0a.log | awk -F'[ ;]+' \
          '{p+=$4; f+=$6; i+=$8} END {print "passed:", p, " failed:", f, " ignored:", i}'
    passed: 31593  failed: 1  ignored: 485

The failure, verbatim, and note that the name in the panic is not the name of the test that
caused it:

    test session::goal_classifier::evidence::tests::changed_files_complete_when_git_diff_exceeds_byte_cap ... FAILED

    thread 'session::goal_classifier::evidence::tests::changed_files_complete_when_git_diff_exceeds_byte_cap' (94157) panicked at crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/session/goal_classifier/evidence.rs:1917:9:
    test premise: the diff must exceed the byte cap

    test result: FAILED. 6876 passed; 1 failed; 32 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 33.23s

    error: test failed, to rerun pass `-p xai-grok-shell --lib`
    ...
    error: 1 target failed:
        `-p xai-grok-shell --lib`

The transcript keeps those lines unwrapped, and they are quoted here as it has them, which is
why they run past the width the rest of this file keeps.

The test that caused it, `baseline_capture_timeout_kills_the_git_it_abandoned` in
`session::goal_classifier::tests`, is green in this very run:

    test session::goal_classifier::tests::baseline_capture_timeout_kills_the_git_it_abandoned ... ok

It set the process-global `GIT_BIN_PATH` to a shim that hung on the call it was aiming at and,
because the shim was not a pass-through, answered every other `git` invocation in the same test
binary for about a second. `git add`, `git commit` and `git diff` then returned exit 0 with
empty stdout, so the victim test built a repository whose diff was zero bytes and its own
premise assertion fired. The mechanism, the reproduction counts and the judgement rule that
came out of it are in `docs/ci-test-debt.md`.

What matters for how to read this section is the pairing: the broken test passed and an
unrelated test failed. A reader who only looked at the name in the failure message would have
fixed the wrong file.

== 5. The CI run of the same push never reached the second failure

`78066cc1` was pushed and CI run `37172407376` went red on the job
`rust check / clippy / test`, for the clippy half of the above:

    error: could not compile `xai-grok-shell` (lib test) due to 2 previous errors

It never ran the tests. In `.github/workflows/ci.yml` clippy is its own step (line 106) and
`cargo test --workspace --locked --no-fail-fast` is a later step (line 146); a failed step ends
the job, so the test step was skipped, and the failure log contains no test results at all:

    $ gh run view 37172407376 --log-failed 2>&1 | grep -cE 'test result: '
    0

`--no-fail-fast` keeps cargo from stopping at the first red test; it does nothing about the
order of workflow steps. The pollution defect was therefore only visible in the container run,
where `cargo clippy` and `cargo test` are two entries in the same `gates=()` array and the
runner keeps going after one of them fails. That is the concrete difference between "CI is
green" and "the container sweep passed" on this repository, and it cuts both ways: here the
container caught something CI could not see.

== 6. Reproducing it, and what this record is not

    $ git clone <this repository> /var/tmp/verify-frozen-8a52ff0a
    $ cd /var/tmp/verify-frozen-8a52ff0a && git checkout --detach 8a52ff0a
    $ IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --full

The clippy leg can be reproduced on its own in that tree with the command the gate runs:

    $ cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings

and the test leg's failure is a race, so it needs the whole `--lib` binary running together.
`cargo test -p xai-grok-shell --lib -- --test-threads=1 --exact` on the two tests named above
passes even on the broken tree; the two tests run concurrently in one binary is the premise.

What this record does not cover:

- It is not the fixed tree. `cargo fmt` and the 32 other gates passed here, but clippy and
  `cargo test` did not, and the two defects were fixed afterwards. Read this as the measurement
  that found them, not as a verdict on the current `main`.
- Four fixtures inside this run declined to check anything, and all four are about a dependency
  the image does not carry: the workflow-yaml cross-check against a real YAML parser
  (`PyYAML is not installed on this host`), the metric-label premise that reads the `unwrap` out
  of the vendored crate (`the prometheus source is not vendored on this host`), and the two
  `timeout children` fixtures that cite the pinned tokio source (`the pinned tokio source is
  not vendored on this machine`). Each is stated with its reason rather than left inside an
  `OK (skipped=1)`, and the equivalent green runs where the dependency is present are recorded
  in `docs/verification/verify-in-docker-full-a04e91a7-2026-10-04.log` section 5 and
  `docs/verification/timeout-child-2026-10-04.log`.
- Linux only. The entry point is a Debian bookworm-slim container; the macOS and Windows
  conclusions come from their own runners, recorded in
  `docs/verification/platform-ci-2026-10-02.log` and
  `docs/verification/windows-test-failures-2026-10-04.log`.
- No browser. Nothing in the 34 gates drives a UI, and the image has no node_modules and no
  browser binary.
